\details{
\code{eyeris} was designed to be extensible, but historically \code{load_asc()} was the
only implemented loader. \code{load_generic()} closes that gap. Native support for
specific tracker formats (e.g., Tobii, SMI, Pupil Labs, GazePoint) can be
layered on top of this function incrementally: a format-specific reader only
needs to produce the standardized data frames documented below and then call
\code{load_generic()}.

The resulting object is structurally identical to one returned by
\code{\link[=load_asc]{load_asc()}}, so it is a drop-in input to \code{\link[=glassbox]{glassbox()}} and the
individual preprocessing steps (\code{\link[=deblink]{deblink()}}, \code{\link[=detransient]{detransient()}},
\code{\link[=interpolate]{interpolate()}}, \code{\link[=lpfilt]{lpfilt()}}, \code{\link[=downsample]{downsample()}},
\code{\link[=bin]{bin()}}, \code{\link[=detrend]{detrend()}}, \code{\link[=zscore]{zscore()}}),
\code{\link[=epoch]{epoch()}}, \code{\link[=bidsify]{bidsify()}}, and the plotting methods.
\subsection{The three standardized data frames}{

Following the conceptual model that most trackers export, \code{load_generic()}
accepts three core data frames (plus an optional fourth for gaze that is
exported separately):
\enumerate{
\item \strong{\code{pupil}} (required) -- the raw sample stream. Must contain a timestamp
column and a pupil-size column. May \emph{also} carry gaze coordinate columns
(\code{eye_x}, \code{eye_y}) if your tracker exports samples as one wide table.
\item \strong{\code{events}} (optional) -- experimental event messages. Must contain a
timestamp column (on the same clock as \code{pupil}) and a message-text column.
Required only if you intend to epoch on event messages later.
\item \strong{\code{blinks}} (optional) -- blink intervals reported by the tracker. Must
contain blink start and end timestamp columns. Note that blink padding via
\code{\link[=deblink]{deblink()}} does \emph{not} depend on this table -- it reconstructs
missing/blink regions directly from \code{NA} (and \code{0}) values in the pupil
column -- so this table is purely for record-keeping and export.
}

A fourth, \strong{\code{gaze}}, data frame is accepted for the less common case where
gaze coordinates are exported separately from pupil size (timestamp + \code{x}/\code{y}
columns); it is joined onto \code{pupil} by timestamp. If your \code{pupil} data frame
already contains gaze columns, leave this \code{NULL}.

If a column in your data frames does not use the default name expected by
\code{load_generic()}, remap it via the \code{mapping} argument (see below) rather than
renaming your data by hand.
}

